  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nChances for off-and-on rain/thunderstorms enter back into the \nforecast by late Tuesday and persist through late Thursday or early \nFriday, as a cold front approaches from the northwest and a deep \nupper trough approaches from the west. Increasing shear coupled with \nmodest instability and strong forcing may support a handful of \nstrong to marginally severe thunderstorms during this time period, \nespecially late Tuesday--Tuesday evening, and again Wednesday--\nWednesday evening. Widespread severe weather appears unlikely at \nthis time. Additionally, well above average precipitable waters and \nthe potential for training echoes will likely support locally heavy \nrainfall and associated minor flooding concerns. \n\nThe main forecast challenge continues to be timing of this storm \nsystem. The ECMWF has consistently been much slower than the GFS and \nCanadian solutions, possibly holding onto precipitation over \nsouthern KS into Friday, with the GFS and Canadian clearing \nprecipitation by Thursday evening as the upper trough phases with a \nsecondary upper trough diving southeast across Mid-America. Looking \nat the latest model trends though, the GFS ensemble mean has been \nsteadily slowing down with this system. Stay tuned as we continue to \nrefine forecast details in the coming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nAbove average heights/thickness will support continued unseasonably \nwarm temperatures through about Tuesday, with daytime highs a few \ndegrees either side of 90 degrees probable. Additionally, stout \nsouth to southwest winds should gust upwards of 30-35 mph through \nMonday, owing to deep and persistent low pressure to the west-\nnorthwest of the region. We continued the trend of increasing wind \nspeeds a bit higher than NBM values.\n\nAs we head into mid-late week, model consensus continues to support \na nice cool down, possibly as early as Wednesday depending on cold \nfrontal timing, as stout Canadian high pressure settles southeast \ninto Mid-America in response to deepening upper troughing over \ncentral and eastern North America. This will likely be the coolest \nair thus far this fall season across the region. \n\nThe GFS and Canadian models continue to support a deeper and further \nwest longwave trough placement and associated cooler temperatures \ncompared to the somewhat warmer ECMWF. For now, a good compromise \nwas daytime highs in the 60s Friday and Saturday and overnight lows \nin the 40s, although this period could turn out to be cooler than \ncurrently forecast, as the NBM is on the warmer end of the\ncurrent model guidance spectrum...stay tuned.",
